desc: Womb Sound Generator v3
// Designed by Rozaya, developed with Claude (Anthropic)
//
// This work is dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal.
// https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
//
// === v3 drift system (nested target selector) ===
//
// v2 had two flat 3-slider drift blocks (Heart drift + Breath drift)
// covering 2 targets total. v3 collapses to 5 drift sliders covering 7
// targets via a target-selector nested-pattern (same pattern resonance
// bank uses). The 7 targets: Heart BPM, S1-S2 gap, Inhale Duration,
// Top Pause, Exhale Duration, Bottom Pause, RSA depth.
//
// Workflow: pick the target you want to drift with slider 54, then set
// up amount / down amount / period / shape on sliders 55-58. The drift
// configuration for THAT target is saved automatically. Pick a different
// target — sliders 55-58 snap to that target's saved values. All seven
// targets' drifts run in parallel; the selector just chooses what
// you're currently editing.
//
// Period units auto-match the target: heartbeats for Heart BPM and
// S1-S2 gap, breath cycles for the four segments and RSA depth.
//
// Drift configurations persist across project save/load via @serialize.
//
// === Sigh mechanism ===
//
// Two new sliders (60, 61) for periodic deep-breath sighs. Set sigh
// interval to ~5 minutes and a depth multiplier; about every N minutes
// one breath's inhale will stretch by the multiplier, then return to
// normal. Models the real sleep-breathing pattern of periodic sighs.
// 0 disables.

// ============================================================================
// SLIDER LAYOUT -- authored in docs/layouts/womb.md, 2026-09-06.
//
// Grouped by LAYER, and each group reads the same way: what it is, how fast it
// goes (value then mode), the shape of its movement, its filtering, its stereo,
// its level, its solo. Then master, transport, drift, ramp -- which is the
// canonical order the rest of the suite already uses.
//
// Two rate PAIRS, not one shared mode. The heart and the breath each carry
// their own rate value and their own mode, adjacent, so either can follow the
// project while the other runs free. That is R20/R21, and it is what the old
// one-entry `Host sync target` picker was a stub for.
// ============================================================================

// --- HEARTBEAT -------------------------------------------------------------
slider1:70<1,1000,1>Heart rate (in heart rate mode units)
// R20/R21. This plugin used to offer TWO options here, `{Own BPM, Host x}`,
// where every other plugin with a rate offers these five -- so anything learned
// about rates elsewhere in the suite was partly untrue here, which is the exact
// thing this sweep exists to stop. `Every N beats` and `N per beat` are
// RECIPROCALS and both are needed: eight cycles per beat should not have to be
// typed as 0.125.
slider2:0<0,4,1{BPM,Seconds,Hz,Every N beats,N per beat}>Heart rate mode
// RSA -- the breath pulling the heart rate up and down. A modifier of the rate
// above, so it is numbered immediately after it.
slider3:0<0,1000,0.001>Heart with breath (BPM peak-to-peak)
// The lub-dub gap. ONE slider serves every unit because typing works (FX box,
// or Tab in the parameter list), so the position count is not a navigation
// problem -- type it, then nudge by ear. The step is what decides which values
// EXIST, and 0.01 reaches everything any of the four units needs.
slider4:120<0.01,1000,0.01>Systole (in systole units)
// Systole unit. This used to be implicit -- milliseconds normally, BEATS under
// sync, with nothing on the control saying so -- and it silently broke a real
// project: `womb-and-baby-heartbeats-with-bloodflow.RPP` held 180 meaning
// milliseconds while the plugin read 180 BEATS, which at its tempo is 154
// seconds against a 0.857-second heartbeat, so S2 never fired at all. A lub
// with no dub. See docs/layouts/womb.md.
//
// The full list rather than a cut-down one, at Rozaya's asking: a stunted
// option list is what we are fixing in the rate mode above, so the gap does not
// get one either.
//
// `% of heartbeat` is NOT called `Cycles`, and that matters. Everywhere else in
// the suite Cycles COUNTS whole cycles -- a drift period of 8 of them. Systole
// is always a fraction of ONE, so calling it Cycles would make the same word
// mean two different things inside one plugin, which is the inconsistency this
// sweep exists to remove. (Rozaya, 2026-09-06: "cycles in fractions? I thought
// cycles were cycles lol" -- correct, and it was named wrong for an hour.) The
// name it takes instead is the idiom already next door in this plugin, where
// Bloodflow Attack and Decay are proportions of a cycle.
//
// It earns its place: systole shortens as the heart speeds up, so a percentage
// is the physiologically right reading and the only unit here that tracks the
// rate instead of fighting it.
slider5:0<0,3,1{Milliseconds,Seconds,Beats,% of heartbeat}>Systole unit
// S1 then S2, each with its own frequency, decay and volume together. These six
// used to be split across three separated pairs, so tuning ONE heart sound
// meant visiting three places in the list.
slider6:45<20,1000,1>S1 Frequency Hz
slider7:60<10,1000,1>S1 Decay ms
slider8:1.0<0.0,1.0,0.01>S1 Volume
slider9:120<60,1000,1>S2 Frequency Hz
slider10:25<5,1000,1>S2 Decay ms
slider11:0.7<0.0,1.0,0.01>S2 Volume
slider12:0.3<0.0,1.0,0.01>Brightness
slider13:3.0<-15.0,15.0,0.1>HB Stereo Width ms (neg = heart right)
slider14:1.0<0.0,1.0,0.01>HB Master Volume
slider15:0<0,1,1{Off,Solo}>Heartbeat Solo

// --- BREATH ----------------------------------------------------------------
// The breath's own rate, and its own mode beside it.
//
// This NO LONGER REWRITES the four segment sliders below. It used to reach in
// and multiply all four so they summed to the rate asked for, calling
// slider_automate on each -- a control whose only job is to overwrite other
// controls, which R20 forbids outright. It never misfired only because every
// saved instance already summed to exactly the right total, so the multiply
// came out as 1.0.
//
// Now: 0 means the cycle is however long the four segments add up to, exactly
// as before. Set, it means the cycle is that long and the four DIVIDE it in
// their stored proportions -- applied when the sound is made, never written
// back. So segments of 4/0/8/0 with this on `Every N beats` = 12 gives four
// beats in and eight beats out, which is Rozaya's own case and the acceptance
// test for this build.
slider16:0<0,1000,0.001>Breaths per minute (0 = the segments below decide)
slider17:0<0,4,1{BPM,Seconds,Hz,Every N beats,N per beat}>Breath rate mode
// The shape of the breath. Their PROPORTIONS divide the cycle when a breath
// rate is set; their absolute values are the cycle when it is not.
slider18:4.0<0.001,1000,0.001>Inhale (seconds, or beats when synced)
slider19:0.3<0,1000,0.01>Top pause (seconds, or beats when synced)
slider20:4.0<0.001,1000,0.001>Exhale (seconds, or beats when synced)
slider21:0.3<0,1000,0.01>Bottom pause (seconds, or beats when synced)
slider22:250<50,2000,1>Inhale Frequency Hz
slider23:170<50,2000,1>Exhale Frequency Hz
slider24:0.3<0.0,1.0,0.01>Inhale Fade In
slider25:0.2<0.0,1.0,0.01>Inhale Fade Out
slider26:0.2<0.0,1.0,0.01>Exhale Fade In
slider27:0.3<0.0,1.0,0.01>Exhale Fade Out
slider28:2<1,4,1{Linear,Cosine,Exponential,Natural}>Fade Mode
slider29:80<20,20000,1>Breath High-pass Hz
slider30:500<50,4000,1>Breath Post-filter Hz
slider31:1.5<0.5,8.0,0.1>Breath Post-filter Q
// Periodic deep breaths. When a sigh fires, the next breath's inhale stretches
// by the depth multiplier (and reads as deeper, because the longer inhale
// envelope rises higher under the same fade shape). 0 interval = no sighs.
slider32:0<0,1000,0.001>Sigh interval (minutes, 0=off)
slider33:1.5<1,1000,0.01>Sigh depth multiplier
slider34:0.5<0.0,1.0,0.01>Breath Stereo Width
slider35:0.5<0.0,1.0,0.01>Breath Volume
slider36:0<0,1,1{Off,Solo}>Breath Solo

// --- BLOODFLOW -------------------------------------------------------------
// Bloodflow has no rate of its own -- it reads the heart's phase directly, one
// line, `bf_cycle_pos = hb_phase / cycle_len`. So the OFFSET takes the rate's
// place at the head of this group: it is the control that says how this layer
// sits in time.
//
// Rozaya, 2026-09-06: "Bloodflow is its own layer, but has no surfaced controls
// to regulate its own rhythm relative to heart." This was flagged and parked in
// June as "locked, open if someone wants decoupling for a real reason"; that
// condition is now met. It needs no phase counter and no memory bank, which is
// what makes it affordable -- the offset is added to the position and wraps.
//
// DEFAULT 0, which is exactly today's behaviour: the flow welded to the thump.
// The offset WRAPS into one heartbeat, so a whole heartbeat of offset is the
// same as none -- which is why its unit is a percentage and not a cycle count.
// 25 puts the flow a quarter of a beat behind the thump; 50 puts it halfway.
slider37:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Bloodflow offset (wraps at one heartbeat)
// SAME LIST AS SYSTOLE, and for the same reason. This said `Cycles` for one
// hour and it was wrong in a way that made the whole control look broken: an
// offset is WRAPPED into one heartbeat, so in cycles every whole number -- 1, 2,
// 5, 10 -- is bit-identical to 0. Only fractions did anything. Rozaya turned the
// control on, moved it to a round number, and correctly heard nothing.
//
// It is the identical error she had already caught on Systole the same day
// ("cycles in fractions? I thought cycles were cycles lol"): Cycles COUNTS whole
// cycles everywhere in this suite, and anything that is really a position inside
// one beat must not borrow the word. `% of heartbeat` is the name, here and
// there, and now every whole number you can type does something.
//
// Milliseconds is the physiologically real one -- pulse transit is roughly 100
// to 250 ms from heart to periphery. Seconds is fixed wall clock, Beats follows
// the project, and `% of heartbeat` scales with the heart rate. Defaulting to
// the percentage because it is the one to reach for first; the offset's own
// default is 0, so the unit changes nothing until it is moved.
slider38:3<0,3,1{Milliseconds,Seconds,Beats,% of heartbeat}>Bloodflow offset unit
slider39:0.15<0.005,0.5,0.005>Bloodflow Attack (proportion of cycle)
slider40:0.85<0.05,0.95,0.01>Bloodflow Decay (proportion of cycle)
slider41:0.1<0,1,0.01>Bloodflow Dicrotic Level
slider42:250<20,2000,1>Bloodflow Filter Hz
slider43:0.2<0.0,0.95,0.01>Bloodflow Resonance
slider44:0.5<0.0,1.0,0.01>Bloodflow Stereo Width
slider45:0.7<0.0,1.0,0.01>Bloodflow Volume
slider46:0<0,1,1{Off,Solo}>Bloodflow Solo

// --- MASTER AND TRANSPORT --------------------------------------------------
slider47:0<0,1,1{Normal,Flipped}>Master Stereo Flip
// Sit silent for this long after playback starts, then begin normally.
slider48:0<0,1000,0.001>Start delay (beats)
// Play/rest gates, per layer, independent. Each counts in its own layer's
// natural unit, which is why they are not one shared pair.
slider49:0<0,1000,1>HB: Play for (beats)
slider50:0<0,1000,1>HB: Rest for (beats)
slider51:0<0,1000,1>Breath: Play for (breaths)
slider52:0<0,1000,1>Breath: Rest for (breaths)
slider53:0<0,1000,1>Bloodflow: Play for (heartbeats)
slider54:0<0,1000,1>Bloodflow: Rest for (heartbeats)

// --- DRIFT (nested-selector pattern, see header) ----------------------------
// Pick a target, then the controls below hold THAT target's drift config.
// Switching the selector saves the current values to the old target's memory
// slot and loads the new target's values in. All targets drift in parallel.
// APPEND to the target list, never insert -- these indices are stored in
// projects AND are the bank position.
slider55:0<0,10,1{Heart rate,S1-S2 gap,Inhale,Top pause,Exhale,Bottom pause,RSA depth,Breaths/min,Inhale Freq,Exhale Freq,Bloodflow offset}>Drift target
slider56:0<0,2000,0.01>Drift up amount (units match target)
slider57:0<0,2000,0.01>Drift down amount (units match target)
slider58:8<1,1000,1>Drift period (in drift period units)
// Declared CYCLES, unlike the Morpher's, because this period already counted
// heartbeats or breath cycles -- Cycles IS the current meaning here, so the
// suite default and the back-compatible one are the same answer.
slider59:0<0,2,1{Cycles,Seconds,Beats}>Drift period unit
slider60:0<0,2,1{Sine,Triangle,Random}>Drift shape
slider61:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift play for (periods, 0 = always)
slider62:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift rest for (periods, 0 = always)

// --- RAMP -------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ramps the selected target by `by` over `duration`, after a start delay.
// `by` is a signed DELTA in the target's own units, not a destination -- 0
// means no ramp, negative means decrease (slower heart, shorter inhale).
// Each target is additive and independent.
slider63:0<0,10,1{Heart rate,S1-S2 gap,Inhale,Top pause,Exhale,Bottom pause,RSA depth,Breaths/min,Inhale Freq,Exhale Freq,Bloodflow offset}>Ramp target
slider64:0<-2000,2000,0.001>Ramp by
// Declared MINUTES, which is both the suite default and what the duration and
// start delay already meant, so no saved instance changes meaning.
slider65:2<0,3,1{Cycles,Seconds,Minutes,Beats}>Ramp time unit
slider66:0<0,1000,0.1>Ramp duration (in ramp time units)
// Turns the smooth ride into a STAIRCASE. The rests come OUT of the duration
// rather than extending it, so a stepped ramp and a smooth one of the same
// length arrive together. Off unless BOTH are above 0.
slider67:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp play for (0 = smooth)
slider68:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp rest for (0 = smooth)
slider69:0<0,1,1{Off,On}>Ramp engage
slider70:0<0,1000,0.1>Ramp start delay (in ramp time units)

@init
// v2.10 render-state fix (Path A — see docs/v2.9-render-state-bug.md).
// NO ext_noinit: REAPER re-runs @init on every transport play / render
// start, resetting all runtime for free (heartbeat/breath/bloodflow state
// machines, oscillators, filters, HB delay buffer, drift runtime, Speed
// Ramp, Start Delay, Play/Rest per layer, BPM smoother, sigh timer, noise
// seeds). Only the drift + Speed Ramp CONFIG banks must survive the
// re-run; they're guarded by drift_cfg_inited below.

// Host tempo capture. `tempo` is READ-ONLY in JSFX; read only, into a
// global deliberately not named `tempo`. Nothing remembers it across play.
host_bpm = max(tempo, 0.001);

srate_inv = 1.0 / srate;
two_pi = 2.0 * $pi;
israte2 = 2.0 / srate;

// --- HEARTBEAT INIT ---
s1_freq = 45.0; s2_freq = 120.0;
s1_phase_osc_near = 0; s1_phase_osc_far = 0;
s2_phase_osc_near = 0; s2_phase_osc_far = 0;

cycle_len_base = floor(srate * 60.0 / 70.0);
cycle_len = cycle_len_base;
systole_samples = floor(srate * 120.0 * 0.001);
sys_unit_sec    = 0.001;
s1_vol = 1.0;
s2_vol = 0.7;
s1_decay = exp(-srate_inv / 0.060);
s2_decay = exp(-srate_inv / 0.025);
s1_attack_rate = exp(-srate_inv / 0.008);
s2_attack_rate = exp(-srate_inv / 0.004);

hb_delay_buf_size = 4096;
memset(0, 0, hb_delay_buf_size);
hb_delay_write = 0;
hb_delay_samples = floor(srate * 0.003);
hb_delay_sign = 1;

hb_phase = 0;
s1_active = 1; s2_active = 0;
s1_env = 1.0; s2_env = 0;
s1_attack_phase = 0; s2_attack_phase = 0;

s1_res_f_near = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 50.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
s1_res_q_near = 2.8; s1_res_low_near = 0; s1_res_band_near = 0;
s2_res_f_near = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 138.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
s2_res_q_near = 2.0; s2_res_low_near = 0; s2_res_band_near = 0;
lp_f_near = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 200.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
lp_low_near = 0; lp_band_near = 0; lp_low2_near = 0; lp_band2_near = 0;

s1_res_f_far = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 58.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
s1_res_q_far = 2.2; s1_res_low_far = 0; s1_res_band_far = 0;
s2_res_f_far = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 150.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
s2_res_q_far = 1.6; s2_res_low_far = 0; s2_res_band_far = 0;
lp_f_far = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 175.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
lp_low_far = 0; lp_band_far = 0; lp_low2_far = 0; lp_band2_far = 0;
lp_q = 0.5;

// --- BREATH INIT ---
breath_state = 0;
breath_state_pos = 0;
breath_state_len = floor(srate * 4.0);

in_lp_l = 0; in_bp_l = 0; in_lp_r = 0; in_bp_r = 0;
ex_lp_l = 0; ex_bp_l = 0; ex_lp_r = 0; ex_bp_r = 0;
hp_lp_l = 0; hp_lp_r = 0;

bpf_low_l = 0; bpf_band_l = 0;
bpf_low_r = 0; bpf_band_r = 0;
bpf_f = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 500.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
bpf_q = 0.5;

in_f_l = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 250.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
in_f_r = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 240.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
ex_f_l = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 170.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
ex_f_r = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 160.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
hp_f   = min(2.0 * sin($pi * 80.0 * srate_inv), 0.98);
breath_lp_q = 0.6;
br_floor = 0.15;

inhale_len = floor(srate * 4.0);
top_len    = floor(srate * 0.3);
exhale_len = floor(srate * 4.0);
bot_len    = floor(srate * 0.3);

// --- BLOODFLOW INIT ---
bf_n3_l = 0; bf_n4_l = 0;
bf_n3_r = 0; bf_n4_r = 0;
bf_filter_hz      = 250.0;
bf_dicrotic_level = 0.1;
bf_res            = 0.2;
bf_attack         = 0.15;
bf_decay          = 0.85;
bf_stereo_width   = 0.5;
bf_floor          = 0.25;

// --- NOISE SEEDS ---
ns1 = 12345; ns2 = 67890; ns3 = 54321;
ns4 = 11111; ns5 = 22222; ns6 = 33333;

function apply_fade(pos, fade_in, fade_out, mode) local(raw, t) (
  raw = 1.0;
  pos < fade_in ? (
    t = pos / fade_in;
    mode == 1 ? raw = t :
    mode == 2 ? raw = 0.5 - 0.5 * cos(t * $pi) :
    mode == 3 ? raw = t * t :
    raw = sin(t * $pi * 0.5);
  ) : pos > (1.0 - fade_out) ? (
    t = (pos - (1.0 - fade_out)) / fade_out;
    mode == 1 ? raw = 1.0 - t :
    mode == 2 ? raw = 0.5 + 0.5 * cos(t * $pi) :
    mode == 3 ? raw = (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) :
    raw = cos(t * $pi * 0.5);
  );
  raw;
);

// Start Delay state.
// (last_play_state removed in v2.10 Path A — no transport-edge handler
// anymore; @init re-running on play is the reset.)
start_delay_elapsed = 0;
start_delay_sec     = 0;
// Seeds for the @block-derived factors, so nothing reads a zero if a block ever
// runs oddly. @block overwrites all of them every block.
beat_sec = 0.5; hb_cycle_sec = 60/70; sys_unit_sec = 0.001; sys_is_pct = 0;
ramp_unit_sec = 60; bf_unit_to_cycles = 1; bf_offset_cycles = 0;
breath_cycle_sec = 0; breath_seg_scale = 1;

// --- Play/Rest state (per-layer, independent) ---
pr_hb_resting    = 0;
pr_hb_beat       = 1;
play_for_hb      = 0;
rest_for_hb      = 0;
pr_hb_enabled    = 0;
this_hb_has_beat = 1;
pr_br_resting            = 0;
pr_br_breath             = 0;
pr_br_rest_elapsed       = 0;
pr_br_rest_duration_samples = 0;
play_for_br              = 0;
rest_for_br              = 0;
pr_br_enabled            = 0;
total_breath_samples     = 0;
pr_bf_resting    = 0;
pr_bf_beat       = 0;
play_for_bf      = 0;
rest_for_bf      = 0;
pr_bf_enabled    = 0;
bf_output_mix    = 1;
pr_smooth = exp(-1 / (srate * 0.003));

// BPM smoother state.
rate_smoother_coeff      = 1.0 - exp(-1.0 / (srate * 0.1));
bpm_target               = slider1;
breath_host_scale = 1;

// last_hs_sel survives ONLY because a 2300000 blob still contains it and the
// stream has to consume it to stay aligned. Nothing reads it. The rest of the
// host-sync trackers are gone with the two-way binding they served.
last_hs_sel = 0;
bpm_smoothed             = slider1;

// Speed ramp state. ramp_t advances 0->1 over slider66 minutes while
// engaged. The target selector (slider63) picks which parameter to ramp;
// only that parameter's effective value gets an additive offset. All
// targets are additive — Heart BPM ramp affects only the heart, not the
// breath cycle (the v2 multiplicative all-three-layers wind-down was
// intentionally NOT carried into v3 — Speed Ramp is for independent
// fine-tuning of one parameter at a time).
// Engage (global) is a freeze/resume gate; only transport play resets the
// per-target ramp clocks. As of v2.14 the ramp is PER-TARGET: each target has
// its own `by`, duration, start delay, and progress clock (banks below).

// Per-target memory bank for stored amounts (signed deltas). Lets the
// user configure an amount for Heart BPM (e.g. -35), switch the selector
// to Inhale, configure an amount there (e.g. +4 sec), and have the
// Heart BPM amount preserved for when they switch back. Same pattern as
// the drift system. 7 targets × 1 value = 7 slots; free at 8320 (after
// the drift 16-slot-aligned blocks ending at 8319). CONFIG — contents
// initialized in the drift_cfg_inited guard below so they survive the
// @init re-run on transport play (Path A).
speed_ramp_target_value_mem = 8320;

// Speed ramp per-target timeline banks (v2.14). duration + start-delay are
// CONFIG (survive transport, restored by @serialize); ramp_t + delay_elapsed
// are RUNTIME (reset every @init / transport play). 16-slot-aligned above the
// `by` bank.
speed_ramp_dur_mem           = 8336;  // duration minutes per target (CONFIG)
speed_ramp_delay_mem         = 8352;  // start-delay minutes per target (CONFIG)
speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem        = 8368;  // ramp progress 0..1 per target (RUNTIME)
speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem = 8384;  // delay sample counter per target (RUNTIME)

// --- Host-sync per-target beats (2026-08-30) ---
// One cycle of each sync target takes this many project beats. CONFIG:
// restored by @serialize, so it survives transport and reload. Slider 64 is
// the editor over it, exactly as Drift's amount sliders edit their banks.
// 16-slot-aligned like the rest, so targets can be APPENDED later -- and they
// will be; two is where this starts, not where it stops.
// CONTENTS are seeded in the drift_cfg_inited guard below, NOT here: this
// plugin has no ext_noinit, so @init re-runs on every transport play, and a
// default written here would reset the heart to one-per-beat and the breath to
// sixteen every time you pressed play. (Which is exactly what it did -- found
// by ear, 2026-08-30. The address itself is fine unguarded; it is a constant.)
// The host-sync target picker is retired -- each rate carries its own mode now,
// so there is nothing to point at. HS_BANK_SLOTS survives because a 2300000
// blob still holds a bank that wide and the stream must consume it to stay
// aligned for whatever follows. Nothing reads the values.
HS_BANK_SLOTS = 2;      // serialised width, frozen so the blob format holds
host_beats_mem = 8400;   // 2 slots. RETIRED as a control (see the slider block)
                         // but still READ from old blobs, because that is where
                         // an un-migrated instance's beats-per-heartbeat lives.

// --- Drift and Ramp play/rest banks (2026-09-06) ---------------------------
// CONFIG (saved) and RUNTIME (not) for the staircase gating, per target, laid
// out on the same 16-slot alignment as everything above. Next free slot: 8544.
drift_play_mem   = 8416;   // CONFIG: play window, in drift PERIODS
drift_rest_mem   = 8432;   // CONFIG: rest window, in drift periods
drift_pr_accum   = 8448;   // RUNTIME
drift_pr_resting = 8464;   // RUNTIME
ramp_play_mem    = 8480;   // CONFIG: play window, in ramp time units
ramp_rest_mem    = 8496;   // CONFIG: rest window
ramp_pr_accum    = 8512;   // RUNTIME
ramp_pr_resting  = 8528;   // RUNTIME

// Play/rest gate, shared by Drift and Ramp. Returns 1 while this target is in
// its REST window, 0 otherwise; ticks the accumulator either way so the two
// windows stay in step. Off entirely unless BOTH windows are above 0 -- one of
// them at 0 must mean "no staircase", not "rest forever". Same implementation
// as the rest of the suite (reference: dapple).
function pr_frozen(accum_bank, resting_bank, idx, tick, play, rest) (
  play > 0 && rest > 0 ? (
    accum_bank[idx] += tick;
    accum_bank[idx] >= (resting_bank[idx] ? rest : play) ? (
      accum_bank[idx] = 0;
      resting_bank[idx] = 1 - resting_bank[idx];
    );
    resting_bank[idx];
  ) : ( 0; );
);

// --- v3 Drift target memory banks ---
//
// 7 targets × 7 fields = 49 slots. Each array is 16-slot-aligned so
// there's room to grow targets later (up to 16) without re-laying-out.
// Memory above hb_delay_buf_size (4096) is free for our use.
//
// Targets:
//   0 = Heart BPM
//   1 = S1-S2 gap (Systole ms)
//   2 = Inhale Duration
//   3 = Top Pause
//   4 = Exhale Duration
//   5 = Bottom Pause
//   6 = RSA depth (Heart with breath BPM peak-to-peak)
//   7 = Breaths/min (aggregate — scales all four breath segments in
//       lockstep, preserving I:E ratio; units = breaths per minute, signed)
//   8 = Inhale Freq (breath-noise filter cutoff for the inhale, Hz)
//   9 = Exhale Freq (breath-noise filter cutoff for the exhale, Hz)
//  10 = Bloodflow offset (how far the pulse sits behind the heart sound, in
//       whatever Bloodflow offset unit says -- added 2026-09-06)
//
// APPEND ONLY. The index is stored in every project (Drift target, Ramp target)
// AND is the bank position, so inserting one silently repoints both.
N_TARGETS = 11;
// The width every bank had before Bloodflow offset was appended. A blob written
// at an older magic carries banks THIS wide, and reading it at N_TARGETS would
// consume one value too many from each and turn everything after the first bank
// into garbage. Six of the nine instances in the library are on such a blob.
OLD_N_TARGETS = 10;
target_drift_up     = 8192;   // [0..6]  configured amount, units match target
target_drift_down   = 8208;   // [0..6]
target_drift_per    = 8224;   // [0..6]  in heartbeats (targets 0,1) or breath cycles (2-6)
target_drift_shape  = 8240;   // [0..6]
target_drift_phase  = 8256;   // [0..6]  runtime: [0, 1) advancing per sample
target_drift_prev   = 8272;   // [0..6]  runtime: Random shape previous random target
target_drift_curr   = 8288;   // [0..6]  runtime: Random shape current random target
target_drift_offset = 8304;   // [0..6]  computed each sample, consumed by audio path

// Initialize all per-target state to zero (no drift, sine shape, fresh phase).
// Note: ext_noinit prevents zeroing on transport play, but @init still runs
// on plugin load — so this initializes a fresh instance. @serialize below
// restores saved configs on project reload.
// CONFIG banks (drift up/down/period/shape + the Speed Ramp `by` bank +
// selector trackers) — initialized ONCE per instance, guarded by
// drift_cfg_inited so the @init re-run on every transport play (Path A
// render fix, no ext_noinit) does NOT wipe the user's setup. @serialize
// below restores saved configs on project reload.
drift_cfg_inited != 1 ? (
  i = 0;
  loop(N_TARGETS,
    speed_ramp_target_value_mem[i] = 0;
    speed_ramp_dur_mem[i]          = 0;
    speed_ramp_delay_mem[i]        = 0;
    i += 1;
  );
  last_speed_target = 0;
  i = 0;
  loop(N_TARGETS,
    target_drift_up[i]    = 0;
    target_drift_down[i]  = 0;
    target_drift_per[i]   = 8;   // heartbeats (targets 0,1) / breath cycles (2-6)
    target_drift_shape[i] = 0;
    drift_play_mem[i]     = 0;   // 0 = no staircase, wander evenly
    drift_rest_mem[i]     = 0;
    ramp_play_mem[i]      = 0;   // 0 = smooth ride
    ramp_rest_mem[i]      = 0;
    i += 1;
  );
  last_target_select = 0;
  // Retired bank, zeroed. Kept only so an old blob's copy has somewhere to land.
  host_beats_mem[0] = 0;
  host_beats_mem[1] = 0;
  drift_cfg_inited = 1;
);

// Drift + speed-ramp RUNTIME (phase / random-shape targets / computed offset /
// ramp clocks) resets on EVERY @init — i.e. every transport play — so
// Sine/Triangle drift restarts at phase 0 (deterministic renders).
i = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i]        = 0;
  speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] = 0;
  drift_pr_accum[i]               = 0;
  drift_pr_resting[i]             = 0;
  ramp_pr_accum[i]                = 0;
  ramp_pr_resting[i]              = 0;
  i += 1;
);
i = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  target_drift_phase[i]  = 0;
  target_drift_prev[i]   = 0;
  target_drift_curr[i]   = 0;
  target_drift_offset[i] = 0;
  i += 1;
);

// One-way BPM rescale state.
last_bpm = slider16;

// --- Sigh mechanism ---
// Pure wall-clock timer (no scaling — 5-minute interval is 5 real minutes
// regardless of Speed Ramp or drift state). When timer crosses interval and
// we're at a state 3 -> 0 transition, sigh_active flips to 1 for the
// full duration of the next breath (all four states). At each state
// transition during a sigh, breath_state_len is set from the CURRENTLY
// drifted segment length × slider33 (depth multiplier) — so the sigh
// breath inherits whatever drift wander is current for each segment,
// scaled by the depth. sigh_active clears at the next 3->0 transition
// (and may re-arm immediately if the timer fires again).
sigh_time_since_last = 0;
sigh_active          = 0;

@slider
// --- Rate modes (R20/R21) --------------------------------------------------
// Two rate PAIRS now, each a value and its own mode, adjacent: the heart
// (sliders 1-2) and the breath (16-17). What used to be here was ONE shared
// two-option mode plus a one-entry target picker plus a separate beats value --
// three controls trying to be one, and a two-way binding between the beats
// value and the heart's BPM readout that had to guess which of them the user
// had moved. All of that is gone. The rate slider IS the value; its mode says
// what the value means; nothing writes into anything.
//
// `Every N beats` and `N per beat` are RECIPROCALS. A test meaning "am I
// host-synced" covers both and is written `>= 3`; a CONVERSION asking "which
// mode am I" must stay exact, because using one formula for both runs the
// plugin at the wrong speed, silently.
rate_mode        = slider2;
breath_rate_mode = slider17;

// --- Nothing converts on a mode change any more -----------------------------
// There used to be a block here that, on entering or leaving Host x, multiplied
// the four breath segments and Systole so they kept the same LENGTH in their
// new unit. That existed because those controls had no unit of their own and
// borrowed the rate mode's. They have their own units now -- `Breath rate mode`
// and `Systole unit` -- so a rate mode change says nothing about them and must
// not touch them. A control that rewrites another control's value is the thing
// R20 forbids, and this was the last of them in this plugin.
//
// The one thing lost with it is the silent landing: switching the heart's mode
// no longer converts the heart's own number either, so the rate value now means
// what the new mode says. That is the suite's behaviour everywhere else, and it
// is the honest one -- the number on screen and the unit beside it always agree.
rate_mode_inited && last_rate_mode != rate_mode ? ( last_rate_mode = rate_mode; );

// --- Nothing hides ---------------------------------------------------------
// Every control here means something in every mode, so there is nothing to
// hide. `Breaths per minute` used to hide in Host x because it REWROTE the four
// segment sliders and that could not be redone on a tempo change; it no longer
// rewrites anything, so it is a plain rate like the heart's and stays visible.
// A hidden control that can still write was the 2026-08-23 bug shape, and the
// safest version of that rule is to have no hidden controls at all.

// bpm_target and systole_samples are DERIVED IN @block, not here: both host
// modes read the live tempo, and a tempo change does not re-run @slider, so a
// value computed here would go stale the moment the project tempo moved.
s1_vol = slider8;
s2_vol = slider11;
s1_freq = slider6; s2_freq = slider9;

s1_center_near = slider6 * 1.1; s2_center_near = slider9 * 1.15;
s1_center_far  = slider6 * 1.28; s2_center_far = slider9 * 1.25;
lp_cutoff_near = 200.0 + slider12 * 250.0;
lp_cutoff_far  = 175.0 + slider12 * 220.0;

s1_res_f_near = min(2.0 * sin($pi * s1_center_near * srate_inv), 0.98);
s2_res_f_near = min(2.0 * sin($pi * s2_center_near * srate_inv), 0.98);
lp_f_near = min(2.0 * sin($pi * lp_cutoff_near * srate_inv), 0.98);
s1_res_f_far = min(2.0 * sin($pi * s1_center_far * srate_inv), 0.98);
s2_res_f_far = min(2.0 * sin($pi * s2_center_far * srate_inv), 0.98);
lp_f_far = min(2.0 * sin($pi * lp_cutoff_far * srate_inv), 0.98);

s1_decay = exp(-srate_inv / (slider7 * 0.001));
s2_decay = exp(-srate_inv / (slider10 * 0.001));

hb_delay_samples = floor(srate * abs(slider13) * 0.001);
hb_delay_samples = min(hb_delay_samples, hb_delay_buf_size - 1);
new_hb_sign = slider13 >= 0 ? 1 : -1;
new_hb_sign != hb_delay_sign ? (
  s1_res_low_near = 0; s1_res_band_near = 0;
  s2_res_low_near = 0; s2_res_band_near = 0;
  lp_low_near = 0; lp_band_near = 0; lp_low2_near = 0; lp_band2_near = 0;
  s1_res_low_far = 0; s1_res_band_far = 0;
  s2_res_low_far = 0; s2_res_band_far = 0;
  lp_low_far = 0; lp_band_far = 0; lp_low2_far = 0; lp_band2_far = 0;
  memset(0, 0, hb_delay_buf_size);
);
hb_delay_sign = new_hb_sign;

// Breaths per minute NO LONGER REWRITES these four. It used to reach in and
// multiply all four so they summed to the rate asked for, calling
// slider_automate on each -- a control whose only job is to overwrite other
// controls, which R20 forbids. It never misfired only because every saved
// instance already summed to exactly the right total, so the ratio came out
// 1.0; and it was HIDDEN in host mode while still able to write, which is the
// 2026-08-23 bug shape. Now the rate scales the ADVANCE in @block instead, and
// these four keep whatever the user typed, forever.
inhale_len = max(floor(srate * slider18), 1);
top_len    = max(floor(srate * slider19), 1);
exhale_len = max(floor(srate * slider20), 1);
bot_len    = max(floor(srate * slider21), 1);

breath_state == 0 ? breath_state_len = inhale_len;
breath_state == 1 ? breath_state_len = top_len;
breath_state == 2 ? breath_state_len = exhale_len;
breath_state == 3 ? breath_state_len = bot_len;
breath_state_pos = min(breath_state_pos, breath_state_len - 1);

in_f_l = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (slider22 * (1.0 + slider34 * 0.15)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
in_f_r = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (slider22 * (1.0 - slider34 * 0.15)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
ex_f_l = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (slider23 * (1.0 + slider34 * 0.12)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
ex_f_r = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (slider23 * (1.0 - slider34 * 0.12)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
hp_f   = min(2.0 * sin($pi * slider29 * srate_inv), 0.98);

// Bloodflow
bf_filter_hz      = slider42;
bf_dicrotic_level = slider41;
bf_res            = slider43;
bf_attack         = max(slider39, 0.001);
bf_decay          = max(slider40, 0.001);
bf_stereo_width   = slider44;

bf_attack + bf_decay > 1.0 ? (
  bf_attack = slider39 / (slider39 + slider40);
  bf_decay  = slider40 / (slider39 + slider40);
);

bpf_f = min(2.0 * sin($pi * slider30 * srate_inv), 0.98);
bpf_q = 1.0 / slider31;

// start_delay_sec is derived in @block -- it needs bpm_target, which follows the
// live tempo in both host modes and so cannot be computed here.

// Play/Rest reads.
play_for_hb   = slider49;
rest_for_hb   = slider50;
pr_hb_enabled = (play_for_hb > 0 && rest_for_hb > 0);

play_for_br      = slider51;
rest_for_br      = slider52;
new_pr_br_enabled = (play_for_br > 0 && rest_for_br > 0);
total_breath_samples = inhale_len + top_len + exhale_len + bot_len;
!new_pr_br_enabled && pr_br_resting ? (
  pr_br_resting    = 0;
  breath_state     = 0;
  breath_state_len = inhale_len;
  breath_state_pos = 0;
);
pr_br_enabled = new_pr_br_enabled;

play_for_bf   = slider53;
rest_for_bf   = slider54;
pr_bf_enabled = (play_for_bf > 0 && rest_for_bf > 0);

// Speed ramp target selector save/restore. slider63 = target; slider64 =
// per-target `by`; slider66 = per-target duration; slider70 = per-target start
// delay (all three per-target as of v2.14). slider69 (engage) stays GLOBAL.
// When the selector changes, save the three per-target sliders to the OLD
// slot, then load the NEW slot's stored values. Otherwise capture live edits.
// Play for / Rest for joined this set on 2026-09-06 and are per-target like
// the rest of it. Ramp time unit and Ramp engage stay GLOBAL: a unit is how you
// read every target's numbers, not a property of one of them.
sel_speed_target = slider63;
last_speed_target != sel_speed_target ? (
  speed_ramp_target_value_mem[last_speed_target] = slider64;
  speed_ramp_dur_mem[last_speed_target]          = slider66;
  speed_ramp_delay_mem[last_speed_target]        = slider70;
  ramp_play_mem[last_speed_target]               = slider67;
  ramp_rest_mem[last_speed_target]               = slider68;
  slider64 = speed_ramp_target_value_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider66 = speed_ramp_dur_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider70 = speed_ramp_delay_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider67 = ramp_play_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider68 = ramp_rest_mem[sel_speed_target];
  slider_automate(slider64);
  slider_automate(slider66);
  slider_automate(slider70);
  slider_automate(slider67);
  slider_automate(slider68);
  last_speed_target = sel_speed_target;
) : (
  speed_ramp_target_value_mem[sel_speed_target] = slider64;
  speed_ramp_dur_mem[sel_speed_target]          = slider66;
  speed_ramp_delay_mem[sel_speed_target]        = slider70;
  ramp_play_mem[sel_speed_target]               = slider67;
  ramp_rest_mem[sel_speed_target]               = slider68;
);

// Speed ramp engage is a freeze/resume gate, NOT a restart trigger.
// While engaged + past start delay, ramp_t advances in @sample.
// Flipping engage Off freezes ramp_t wherever it is; flipping On
// resumes from that position. The only thing that resets ramp_t is a
// transport play edge (handled in @sample). This means a slider change
// while engaged — including switching the target selector — does NOT
// restart the ramp.

// --- v3 Drift target save/restore ---
//
// Detect target-selector change. If the user just switched targets,
// save the current sliders 55-58 into the OLD target's memory slot,
// then load the NEW target's saved values into sliders 55-58.
// If the target didn't change, write the current slider values into
// the current target's slot (so edits are captured live).
sel_target = slider55;
sel_up     = slider56;
sel_down   = slider57;
sel_per    = slider58;
sel_shape  = slider60;
sel_dplay  = slider61;
sel_drest  = slider62;

last_target_select != sel_target ? (
  // Save current sliders into the OLD target's slot
  target_drift_up[last_target_select]    = sel_up;
  target_drift_down[last_target_select]  = sel_down;
  target_drift_per[last_target_select]   = sel_per;
  target_drift_shape[last_target_select] = sel_shape;
  drift_play_mem[last_target_select]     = sel_dplay;
  drift_rest_mem[last_target_select]     = sel_drest;

  // Load the NEW target's saved values into the sliders.
  slider56 = target_drift_up[sel_target];
  slider57 = target_drift_down[sel_target];
  slider58 = target_drift_per[sel_target];
  slider60 = target_drift_shape[sel_target];
  slider61 = drift_play_mem[sel_target];
  slider62 = drift_rest_mem[sel_target];
  slider_automate(slider56);
  slider_automate(slider57);
  slider_automate(slider58);
  slider_automate(slider60);
  slider_automate(slider61);
  slider_automate(slider62);

  last_target_select = sel_target;
) : (
  // Target didn't change — capture live edits into the current slot.
  target_drift_up[sel_target]    = sel_up;
  target_drift_down[sel_target]  = sel_down;
  target_drift_per[sel_target]   = sel_per;
  target_drift_shape[sel_target] = sel_shape;
  drift_play_mem[sel_target]     = sel_dplay;
  drift_rest_mem[sel_target]     = sel_drest;
);

// Breath-frequency target activity flag (v2.14 Job C — breath frequencies).
// True when Inhale Freq (target 8) or Exhale Freq (target 9) has any Drift or
// Speed Ramp configured. Gates the per-sample breath-filter-coeff recompute in
// @sample so the common case (no breath-freq target) keeps the coeffs cached
// here and pays nothing. When a freq target is un-configured, this @slider
// re-run also resets in_f_l/r + ex_f_l/r back to their base (below).
// breath_freq_active is DERIVED from the drift and Speed Ramp banks, so it is
// computed in @block instead -- see the note there.

@block
// --- Restore-order guard for the change-detecting controls below -----------
//     These trackers answer "did the user just move this control?". Adopted
//     HERE, not in @slider: on a fresh or duplicated instance @slider runs
//     while REAPER is still handing over the saved values, so a tracker
//     adopted there takes a DEFAULT, and the real value arriving a moment
//     later reads as a user edit -- the picker then fires and slider_automate()s
//     its ratio over a rate that was set by hand. @block cannot run before the
//     instance is configured, so adopting here is right whatever order the
//     restore paths ran in. (Proved 2026-08-23: a Sweeping Filter in BPM mode
//     with Host ratio "every 2 beats" had Rate Value stamped to 0.5 on every
//     load and every track duplicate.)
!rate_mode_inited ? ( last_rate_mode = slider2; rate_mode_inited = 1; );

// --- The old-blob seeding path is GONE, and this is where it was -----------
// It used to convert, on load, an instance whose blob predated the beats bank:
// heart rate from a tempo multiplier into BPM, and the breath segments and
// Systole from seconds/milliseconds into beats. It fired only for instances in
// Host x, which in the whole library is one -- `womb-and-baby-heartbeats-with-
// bloodflow.RPP`, whose Systole of 180 it quietly turned into 0.21 beats (180
// ms at its tempo) on every open. That instance was never broken; this block is
// why.
//
// It is removed because the 2026-09-06 layout migration does the same
// conversion ONCE, in the project file, where it can be checked. Leaving both
// would convert twice. `tools/womb_migrate_layout_20260906.py` carries the
// arithmetic verbatim and names the one instance it applies to.
//
// The blob is still READ for its drift and ramp banks -- see @serialize, which
// reads an old blob at the OLD bank width. Six of the nine instances in the
// library are on such a blob, so that path is load-bearing and is not going
// anywhere.

// --- Gating flag DERIVED from the @serialize banks, and therefore computed
//     here rather than in @slider.
//
// It gates the per-sample breath-filter-coeff recompute below, so the common
// case (no breath-freq target configured) keeps the coeffs cached and pays
// nothing. In @slider it was subject to the restore-order bug: @slider and
// @serialize have no guaranteed order, so on a load or track duplicate where
// @slider ran first this was computed from empty banks, came out 0, and was
// never recomputed once the banks were restored underneath it -- a configured
// breath-frequency drift would simply not move until a control was touched.
// @block runs every block regardless of what happened when. (Same bug found
// in the Morpher on 2026-08-19 and swept across the suite.)
breath_freq_active = (target_drift_up[8] > 0 || target_drift_down[8] > 0 ||
                      target_drift_up[9] > 0 || target_drift_down[9] > 0 ||
                      speed_ramp_target_value_mem[8] != 0 ||
                      speed_ramp_target_value_mem[9] != 0);

// --- The two rate chains, and every unit factor ----------------------------
// ALL of this is derived here, per block, and none of it in @slider: both host
// modes read the LIVE tempo, and a tempo change does not re-run @slider, so
// anything computed there would go stale the moment the project tempo moved.
//
// R21, and the distinction that shipped inverted in two other plugins: a GATE
// meaning "am I host-synced" is `>= 3` and covers both host modes; a CONVERSION
// asking "which mode am I" must stay EXACT, because `Every N beats` and
// `N per beat` are reciprocals and one formula for both runs at the wrong
// speed, silently. Every chain below spells out both host arms.
beat_sec = 60 / max(tempo, 0.001);

// HEART. The slider is the rate value; the mode says what it means. The result
// is beats per minute, which is what the whole heartbeat engine already reads.
hr_raw = max(slider1, 0.001);
bpm_target =
  rate_mode == 0 ? hr_raw :                       // BPM, the identity
  rate_mode == 1 ? 60 / hr_raw :                  // Seconds per beat
  rate_mode == 2 ? hr_raw * 60 :                  // Hz
  rate_mode == 3 ? 60 / (hr_raw * beat_sec) :     // Every N beats per heartbeat
                   60 * hr_raw / beat_sec;        // N heartbeats per beat
bpm_target = max(0.01, min(1000, bpm_target));

// BREATH. Its own value and its own mode, so the breath can follow the project
// while the heart runs free, or the reverse. 0 means "no rate set": the cycle
// is however long the four segments add up to, exactly as it always was.
//
// The engine consumes breath_cycle_sec (0 = let the segments decide) and
// breath_host_scale. The four segments keep their stored numbers ALWAYS -- this
// no longer rewrites them, which is what `Breaths per minute` used to do.
br_raw = slider16;
breath_cycle_sec =
  br_raw <= 0        ? 0 :                            // segments decide
  breath_rate_mode == 0 ? 60 / max(br_raw, 0.001) :   // breaths per minute
  breath_rate_mode == 1 ? max(br_raw, 0.001) :        // seconds per breath
  breath_rate_mode == 2 ? 1 / max(br_raw, 0.001) :    // Hz
  breath_rate_mode == 3 ? max(br_raw, 0.001) * beat_sec :  // Every N beats
                          beat_sec / max(br_raw, 0.001);   // N per beat
// The segments are read in the breath's own unit. Under either host mode they
// are BEATS, so the state machine advances tempo/60 samples per sample and a
// 4-beat inhale is four beats at any tempo. Otherwise they are seconds and it
// advances one for one.
breath_host_scale = breath_rate_mode >= 3 ? max(tempo, 0.001) / 60 : 1;

// SYSTOLE. Its own unit control since 2026-09-06, so the rate mode no longer
// says anything about it. `% of heartbeat` is a proportion of one cycle, which
// is why it is the one option resolved against the heart rather than the clock.
sys_unit_sec =
  slider5 == 0 ? 0.001 :                      // milliseconds
  slider5 == 1 ? 1 :                          // seconds
  slider5 == 2 ? beat_sec :                   // beats
                 60 / max(bpm_target, 0.01);  // % of heartbeat: one whole cycle
sys_is_pct = slider5 == 3;

// RAMP TIME unit. (The DRIFT period unit is applied inside the drift loop
// instead, because its `Cycles` is per-target -- heartbeats for the heart-side
// targets, breath cycles for the breath ones -- so there is no single factor
// to compute here. Same option list, same meaning, different arithmetic.)
hb_cycle_sec   = 60 / max(bpm_target, 0.01);
ramp_unit_sec  = slider65 == 0 ? hb_cycle_sec :
                 slider65 == 1 ? 1 :
                 slider65 == 2 ? 60 : beat_sec;

// BLOODFLOW OFFSET: how many CYCLES one unit of the offset control is worth,
// since bf_cycle_pos is a fraction of a cycle. A factor rather than a finished
// offset, because this is a Drift and Ramp target too and those offsets arrive
// per sample. Cycles is already the answer; the other two are lengths of time
// divided by the cycle.
bf_unit_to_cycles =
  slider38 == 0 ? 0.001 / max(hb_cycle_sec, 0.0001) :   // milliseconds
  slider38 == 1 ? 1 / max(hb_cycle_sec, 0.0001) :       // seconds
  slider38 == 2 ? beat_sec / max(hb_cycle_sec, 0.0001) :// beats
                  0.01;                                 // % of one heartbeat

// The breath rate, applied. The four segments keep their stored numbers; what
// changes is how fast the state machine walks through them. natural_cycle_sec
// is how long they take on their own; the scale is the ratio to the requested
// length, so at the setting where they already sum to it the scale is exactly
// 1 and nothing moves.
//
// Worked, because it is the acceptance test: segments 4/0/8/0 with the breath
// on `Every N beats` = 12 at tempo 70. breath_host_scale is 70/60, so the
// natural cycle is 12/(70/60) = 10.286 s; the requested cycle is 12*60/70 =
// 10.286 s; the scale is 1; the inhale is 4 beats and the exhale is 8. Four
// beats in, eight beats out, which is what Rozaya asked to keep.
seg_sum_units     = slider18 + slider19 + slider20 + slider21;
natural_cycle_sec = max(seg_sum_units, 0.001) / max(breath_host_scale, 0.0001);
breath_seg_scale  = breath_cycle_sec > 0
  ? natural_cycle_sec / breath_cycle_sec
  : 1;
breath_host_scale = breath_host_scale * breath_seg_scale;

// Start delay counts HEARTBEATS, as its name says, in every mode.
start_delay_sec = slider48 * 60 / max(bpm_target, 0.001);

@sample

// Smooth BPM toward bpm_target.
bpm_smoothed += (bpm_target - bpm_smoothed) * rate_smoother_coeff;

// Advance each target's own ramp clock (v2.14 per-target timeline). Engage
// (slider69, GLOBAL) is the freeze/resume gate: while On, each target whose
// duration > 0 advances its own ramp_t 0->1 over its own duration, after its
// own start delay; while Off, all clocks freeze and resume. Only transport
// play (via @init) resets them.
// Ramp time unit (slider65) replaces the hard 60 that used to make all three
// of these read in minutes: the duration, the start delay and the play/rest
// windows, together, which is why one control covers the four of them.
slider69 ? (
  i = 0;
  loop(N_TARGETS,
    speed_ramp_dur_mem[i] > 0 ? (
      sr_delay_samples = speed_ramp_delay_mem[i] * ramp_unit_sec * srate;
      speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] < sr_delay_samples ? (
        speed_ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] += 1;
      ) : (
        // Ramp play/rest -- "ramp on one side, stairs on the other". The rests
        // come OUT of the duration rather than extending it: while moving it
        // moves faster, by exactly (play+rest)/play, so a stepped ride and a
        // smooth one of the same length land together. Off unless BOTH are > 0.
        rpl = ramp_play_mem[i];
        rrs = ramp_rest_mem[i];
        rhold = pr_frozen(ramp_pr_accum, ramp_pr_resting, i,
                          1.0 / max(ramp_unit_sec * srate, 1), rpl, rrs);
        !rhold ? (
          rstep = 1.0 / max(srate * speed_ramp_dur_mem[i] * ramp_unit_sec, 1);
          (rpl > 0 && rrs > 0) ? rstep *= (rpl + rrs) / rpl;
          speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i] += rstep;
          speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i] > 1.0 ? speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[i] = 1.0;
        );
      );
    );
    i += 1;
  );
);

// Compute per-target Speed Ramp effect. ALL 7 targets ramp in parallel
// (same shape as drift — see the drift loop below). Each target's offset
// is `ramp_t * stored_amount` where the stored amount comes from the
// per-target memory bank, not from slider64. The @slider block keeps the
// bank in sync with slider64 for the currently-selected target (live
// edits captured; non-selected targets stay at their saved amounts).
// This way switching the selector doesn't stop any ramp — the selector
// just changes which target's amount you're currently editing.
// Each target is additive: a Heart BPM ramp affects only the heart;
// breath cycle stays at its base rate unless the operator also configures
// Speed Ramp on a breath segment.
speed_ramp_heart_offset   = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[0] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[0];
speed_ramp_systole_offset = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[1] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[1];
speed_ramp_inhale_offset  = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[2] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[2];
speed_ramp_top_offset     = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[3] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[3];
speed_ramp_exhale_offset  = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[4] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[4];
speed_ramp_bot_offset     = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[5] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[5];
speed_ramp_rsa_offset     = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[6] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[6];
speed_ramp_bpm_offset     = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[7] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[7];
speed_ramp_in_freq_offset = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[8] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[8];
speed_ramp_ex_freq_offset = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[9] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[9];
speed_ramp_bf_off_offset  = speed_ramp_ramp_t_mem[10] * speed_ramp_target_value_mem[10];

// --- v3 Per-target Drift wave computation ---
//
// Each of 7 targets gets a phase counter advancing per sample. Period
// units differ by target: heartbeats for Heart BPM and S1-S2 gap,
// breath cycles for the four breath segments and RSA depth. Shape +
// up/down amplitudes are read from per-target memory.
//
// The resulting offset is stored back into target_drift_offset[t] and
// consumed later in @sample where each target is applied to its audio
// path parameter (BPM, systole_samples, segment lengths, RSA depth).
i = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  up    = target_drift_up[i];
  down  = target_drift_down[i];
  per   = target_drift_per[i];
  shape = target_drift_shape[i];

  drift_offset_local = 0;
  (up > 0 || down > 0) && per > 0 ? (
    // Period base: heartbeats for targets 0,1; breath cycles for 2-6.
    // Period base: heartbeats for targets 0,1; breath cycles for 2-6. The
    // breath side divides by breath_host_scale because total_breath_samples is
    // the length the four sliders describe, NOT the length actually played --
    // in Host x the cycle is consumed at a different rate, so without this a
    // drift period of "3 breath cycles" would be three cycles of a breath that
    // is not the one you can hear. Heart periods need no equivalent: their base
    // is cycle_len_base, which already scales. (1 in Own BPM, so untouched.)
    // Drift period unit (slider59). CYCLES is what this always counted, and it
    // is per-target: heartbeats for the heart-side targets, breath cycles for
    // the breath ones. Seconds and Beats are wall clock and project tempo, and
    // are the same for every target because they are not cycles of anything.
    period_base = slider59 == 1 ? srate :
                  slider59 == 2 ? srate * beat_sec :
                  i < 2 ? max(cycle_len_base, 1)
                        : max(total_breath_samples / max(breath_host_scale, 0.0001), 1);
    period_samples = max(per * period_base, 1);

    // Drift play/rest. Windows are whole PERIODS. While resting the phase is
    // held, so the offset FREEZES where it stopped rather than returning to
    // centre -- the staircase, not a reset.
    d_frozen = pr_frozen(drift_pr_accum, drift_pr_resting, i,
                         1.0 / period_samples,
                         drift_play_mem[i], drift_rest_mem[i]);
    // One-armed on purpose: an empty else-branch is a silent compile failure.
    d_frozen ? ( new_phase = target_drift_phase[i]; );
    !d_frozen ? (
      new_phase = target_drift_phase[i] + 1.0 / period_samples;
      new_phase >= 1.0 ? (
        new_phase -= 1.0;
        shape == 2 ? (
          target_drift_prev[i] = target_drift_curr[i];
          target_drift_curr[i] = rand(2) - 1;
        );
      );
      target_drift_phase[i] = new_phase;
    );

    shape == 0 ? (
      shape_val = sin(2 * $pi * new_phase);
    ) : shape == 1 ? (
      shape_val = new_phase < 0.25 ? (4 * new_phase) :
                  new_phase < 0.75 ? (2 - 4 * new_phase) :
                                     (4 * new_phase - 4);
    ) : (
      shape_val = target_drift_prev[i] +
        (target_drift_curr[i] - target_drift_prev[i]) * new_phase;
    );
    drift_offset_local = shape_val > 0 ? shape_val * up : shape_val * down;
  );
  target_drift_offset[i] = drift_offset_local;
  i += 1;
);

// Heart-with-breath (RSA) modulation — coupled to breath state, not
// to its own phase. Drift target 6 wanders the baseline depth (slider3);
// Speed Ramp target 6 (RSA depth) ramps it toward the configured target
// over the duration. Both add as offsets.
rsa_baseline_bpm = max(slider3 + target_drift_offset[6] + speed_ramp_rsa_offset, 0);
heart_with_breath_offset = 0;
rsa_baseline_bpm > 0 ? (
  rsa_half = rsa_baseline_bpm * 0.5;
  breath_phase_pos_for_rsa = breath_state_pos / max(breath_state_len - 1, 1);
  breath_state == 0 ? heart_with_breath_offset = (breath_phase_pos_for_rsa - 0.5) * rsa_baseline_bpm :
  breath_state == 1 ? heart_with_breath_offset = rsa_half :
  breath_state == 2 ? heart_with_breath_offset = (0.5 - breath_phase_pos_for_rsa) * rsa_baseline_bpm :
                      heart_with_breath_offset = -rsa_half;
);

// Apply heart-side modulation: BPM drift (target 0) + RSA + Speed Ramp
// heart offset. All additive in raw BPM.
// Floor lowered from 20 to 0.01 -- see the heartbeat note; a silent clamp
// would defeat Host x at small multipliers.
// Drift and Speed Ramp offsets are in the plugin's OWN unit in every mode --
// BPM here, never a multiplier, even in Host x. Tried it the other way and it
// fails on the slider grid: these amount sliders step in 0.1, chosen for BPM,
// and in multiplier terms 0.1 is a 10% wander with nothing finer reachable.
// The value you want stops being settable, which is worse than the
// inconsistency it fixed. Consequence, deliberate and documented: a ramp does
// NOT stretch when the project tempo changes -- -60 stays -60 BPM.
effective_bpm  = max(bpm_smoothed + target_drift_offset[0] + heart_with_breath_offset + speed_ramp_heart_offset, 0.01);
cycle_len_base = floor(srate * 60.0 / effective_bpm);

// Apply systole drift (target 1) + Speed Ramp offset: baseline ms + drift + ramp.
// Drift and Speed Ramp offsets are in the slider's own unit, so they ride the
// conversion with it. The floor is 0.001 of a unit rather than 1: in Host x a
// floor of 1 would be a whole beat.
effective_systole = max(slider4 + target_drift_offset[1] + speed_ramp_systole_offset, 0.001);
// `% of heartbeat` is a PROPORTION of one cycle, so it divides by 100 first;
// sys_unit_sec for that mode is the whole cycle in seconds.
systole_samples = floor(srate * effective_systole *
                        (sys_is_pct ? 0.01 : 1) * sys_unit_sec);
// CLAMP, and it is not cosmetic. S2 fires on `hb_phase == systole_samples`, and
// hb_phase wraps at cycle_len -- so a systole at or past the end of the cycle is
// never reached and S2 NEVER SOUNDS AT ALL. Silent, not odd: a lub with no dub.
// Nothing in the library reaches that state, and the point of this line is that
// nothing can. Held one sample short of the wrap so the equality is reachable.
systole_samples = max(1, min(systole_samples, max(cycle_len_base, 2) - 1));

// Breaths/min aggregate (target 7): a proportional scale across ALL four
// segments in lockstep, preserving the I:E ratio — the ratio-based
// aggregate spec'd for Job B. Combined breath-rate delta (drift + Speed
// Ramp, in breaths/min) shifts the whole breath rate; each segment's
// effective duration is then divided by the scale (faster rate = shorter
// segments). Composes with the per-segment drift + ramp offsets below.
base_breath_total = max(slider18 + slider19 + slider20 + slider21, 0.001);
base_breath_rate  = 60 / base_breath_total;
breath_rate_delta = target_drift_offset[7] + speed_ramp_bpm_offset;
breath_rate_scale = max(base_breath_rate + breath_rate_delta, 0.001) / base_breath_rate;

// Apply breath segment drifts (targets 2-5) + Speed Ramp offsets, then the
// Breaths/min aggregate scale. Drift wanders each segment cycle-to-cycle;
// Speed Ramp adds a one-way movement over the configured duration; the
// aggregate scales the whole breath rate.
effective_inhale_sec = max((slider18 + target_drift_offset[2] + speed_ramp_inhale_offset) / breath_rate_scale, 0.01);
effective_top_sec    = max((slider19 + target_drift_offset[3] + speed_ramp_top_offset)    / breath_rate_scale, 0);
effective_exhale_sec = max((slider20 + target_drift_offset[4] + speed_ramp_exhale_offset) / breath_rate_scale, 0.01);
effective_bot_sec    = max((slider21 + target_drift_offset[5] + speed_ramp_bot_offset)    / breath_rate_scale, 0);
inhale_len = max(floor(srate * effective_inhale_sec), 1);
top_len    = max(floor(srate * effective_top_sec), 1);
exhale_len = max(floor(srate * effective_exhale_sec), 1);
bot_len    = max(floor(srate * effective_bot_sec), 1);

// Breath-frequency Drift + Speed Ramp (targets 8, 9). When configured, the
// inhale/exhale breath-noise filter cutoffs (in_f_l/r, ex_f_l/r — cached in
// @slider) are recomputed per sample from the effective frequency (base slider
// + drift offset + ramp offset), so the breath brightens/darkens live. Same
// formula + stereo-width spread as the @slider base computation, on the drifted
// value. Gated by breath_freq_active so unused instances keep the cached coeffs
// and pay nothing. Clamped to 20 Hz so a big negative offset can't invert sin().
breath_freq_active ? (
  eff_in_freq = max(slider22 + target_drift_offset[8] + speed_ramp_in_freq_offset, 20);
  eff_ex_freq = max(slider23 + target_drift_offset[9] + speed_ramp_ex_freq_offset, 20);
  in_f_l = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (eff_in_freq * (1.0 + slider34 * 0.15)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
  in_f_r = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (eff_in_freq * (1.0 - slider34 * 0.15)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
  ex_f_l = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (eff_ex_freq * (1.0 + slider34 * 0.12)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
  ex_f_r = min(2.0 * sin($pi * (eff_ex_freq * (1.0 - slider34 * 0.12)) * srate_inv), 0.98);
);

// Note: the sigh multiplier (slider33) is NOT applied here. It's
// applied at state transitions where breath_state_len is set —
// see the state machine block below. That way drift continues to
// wander each segment's length per sample (this block), and at the
// MOMENT a sigh state starts, the currently-drifted segment length
// is multiplied by slider33 to set breath_state_len. The result is
// "sigh inherits live drift" without storing any extra state.

// Recompute total breath samples after drift, used by Breath rest timer
// and by breath drift periods.
total_breath_samples = inhale_len + top_len + exhale_len + bot_len;

// Breath state_pos advances at 1× sample-rate in Own BPM; segment lengths
// carry any drift or Speed Ramp offset. v3 dropped v2's combined_scale
// multiplicative shape — each target's effect is now contained to its
// own parameter via additive offsets above.
// In Host x it advances at the project's rate instead (see @block), so the
// breath follows the tempo exactly as the heart does. Everything counted in
// breath-time rides this: the Play/Rest timer below already uses it, and the
// segment lengths scale by being CONSUMED faster rather than being rewritten,
// so drift and Speed Ramp offsets survive untouched. The sigh timer
// deliberately does not -- it is wall-clock minutes and stays that way.
breath_state_advance = breath_host_scale;

// Sigh timer advances in wall-clock seconds. Pure wall-clock with no
// scaling: a 5-minute sigh interval is 5 real minutes, regardless of
// what Speed Ramp or drift are doing.
slider32 > 0 ? (
  sigh_time_since_last += 1.0 / srate;
);

// (No transport-edge reset here — Path A. REAPER re-runs @init on every
// transport play / render start, which resets all runtime state — all
// three layer state machines, oscillators, filters, HB delay buffer,
// drift runtime (phase 0 → deterministic renders), Speed Ramp, Start
// Delay, Play/Rest per layer, BPM smoother, sigh timer, noise seeds; the
// CONFIG banks are preserved across those re-runs by the drift_cfg_inited
// guard in @init. See docs/v2.9-render-state-bug.md.)

start_delay_elapsed < start_delay_sec ? (
  start_delay_elapsed += 1 / srate;
  spl0 = 0;
  spl1 = 0;
) : (

// --- NOISE ---
// White noise via Park-Miller / MINSTD (see CLAUDE.md gotcha: EEL2 float64
// rounds an LCG multiply once a*seed exceeds 2^53 — the old 22695477
// streams ns2/ns4/ns6, one per layer's decorrelated channel, overflowed
// into a periodic tone + DC offset on R). Three different multipliers
// (48271 / 69621 / 16807) are spread so each summed output channel
// (L: n1/n3/n5, R: n2/n4/n6) draws from three independent streams. Seeds
// (set in @init) are distinct nonzero values in [1, 2^31-2].
ns1 = (ns1 * 48271) % 2147483647; n1 = ns1 * (2.0 / 2147483647.0) - 1.0;
ns2 = (ns2 * 69621) % 2147483647; n2 = ns2 * (2.0 / 2147483647.0) - 1.0;
ns3 = (ns3 * 16807) % 2147483647; n3 = ns3 * (2.0 / 2147483647.0) - 1.0;
ns4 = (ns4 * 48271) % 2147483647; n4 = ns4 * (2.0 / 2147483647.0) - 1.0;
ns5 = (ns5 * 69621) % 2147483647; n5 = ns5 * (2.0 / 2147483647.0) - 1.0;
ns6 = (ns6 * 16807) % 2147483647; n6 = ns6 * (2.0 / 2147483647.0) - 1.0;

// --- HEARTBEAT CYCLE LENGTH ---
// Cap raised from 200 to 1000 BPM for the same reason.
cycle_len = max(cycle_len_base, floor(srate * 60.0 / 1000.0));

// --- HEARTBEAT ---
hb_phase += 1;
hb_phase >= cycle_len ? (
  hb_phase = 0;
  pr_hb_enabled ? (
    pr_hb_beat >= (pr_hb_resting ? rest_for_hb : play_for_hb) ? (
      pr_hb_resting = 1 - pr_hb_resting;
      pr_hb_beat = 0;
    );
    pr_hb_beat += 1;
  );
  pr_bf_enabled ? (
    pr_bf_beat >= (pr_bf_resting ? rest_for_bf : play_for_bf) ? (
      pr_bf_resting = 1 - pr_bf_resting;
      pr_bf_beat = 0;
    );
    pr_bf_beat += 1;
  );
  this_hb_has_beat = !pr_hb_enabled || !pr_hb_resting;
  this_hb_has_beat ? (
    s1_active = 1; s1_env = 0.0; s1_attack_phase = 1;
  );
);
hb_phase == systole_samples && this_hb_has_beat ? (
  s2_active = 1; s2_env = 0.0; s2_attack_phase = 1;
);

s1_active ? (
  s1_attack_phase ? (
    s1_env += (1.0 - s1_env) * (1.0 - s1_attack_rate);
    s1_env >= 0.98 ? s1_attack_phase = 0;
  ) : (
    s1_env *= s1_decay;
    s1_env < 0.0001 ? s1_active = 0;
  );
) : 0;

s2_active ? (
  s2_attack_phase ? (
    s2_env += (1.0 - s2_env) * (1.0 - s2_attack_rate);
    s2_env >= 0.98 ? s2_attack_phase = 0;
  ) : (
    s2_env *= s2_decay;
    s2_env < 0.0001 ? s2_active = 0;
  );
) : 0;

s1_phase_osc_near += two_pi * s1_freq * srate_inv;
s1_phase_osc_near > two_pi ? s1_phase_osc_near -= two_pi;
s2_phase_osc_near += two_pi * s2_freq * srate_inv;
s2_phase_osc_near > two_pi ? s2_phase_osc_near -= two_pi;
s1_phase_osc_far += two_pi * (s1_freq * 1.003) * srate_inv;
s1_phase_osc_far > two_pi ? s1_phase_osc_far -= two_pi;
s2_phase_osc_far += two_pi * (s2_freq * 0.997) * srate_inv;
s2_phase_osc_far > two_pi ? s2_phase_osc_far -= two_pi;

s1_active ? (
  s1_res_low_near += s1_res_f_near * s1_res_band_near;
  s1_res_high_near = (sin(s1_phase_osc_near)*0.8 + n1*0.2) - s1_res_low_near - s1_res_q_near * s1_res_band_near;
  s1_res_band_near += s1_res_f_near * s1_res_high_near;
  s1_out_near = s1_res_band_near * s1_env * s1_env * 2.0 * s1_vol;
) : s1_out_near = 0;
s2_active ? (
  s2_res_low_near += s2_res_f_near * s2_res_band_near;
  s2_res_high_near = (sin(s2_phase_osc_near)*0.5 + n1*0.5) - s2_res_low_near - s2_res_q_near * s2_res_band_near;
  s2_res_band_near += s2_res_f_near * s2_res_high_near;
  s2_out_near = s2_res_band_near * s2_env * s2_env * s2_vol;
) : s2_out_near = 0;

raw_near = s1_out_near + s2_out_near;
lp_low_near += lp_f_near * lp_band_near;
lp_high_near = raw_near - lp_low_near - lp_q * lp_band_near;
lp_band_near += lp_f_near * lp_high_near;
lp_low2_near += lp_f_near * lp_band2_near;
lp_high2_near = lp_low_near - lp_low2_near - lp_q * lp_band2_near;
lp_band2_near += lp_f_near * lp_high2_near;
heart_near = lp_low2_near * 1.5;

s1_active ? (
  s1_res_low_far += s1_res_f_far * s1_res_band_far;
  s1_res_high_far = (sin(s1_phase_osc_far)*0.8 + n2*0.2) - s1_res_low_far - s1_res_q_far * s1_res_band_far;
  s1_res_band_far += s1_res_f_far * s1_res_high_far;
  s1_out_far = s1_res_band_far * s1_env * s1_env * 2.0 * s1_vol;
) : s1_out_far = 0;
s2_active ? (
  s2_res_low_far += s2_res_f_far * s2_res_band_far;
  s2_res_high_far = (sin(s2_phase_osc_far)*0.5 + n2*0.5) - s2_res_low_far - s2_res_q_far * s2_res_band_far;
  s2_res_band_far += s2_res_f_far * s2_res_high_far;
  s2_out_far = s2_res_band_far * s2_env * s2_env * s2_vol;
) : s2_out_far = 0;

raw_far = s1_out_far + s2_out_far;
lp_low_far += lp_f_far * lp_band_far;
lp_high_far = raw_far - lp_low_far - lp_q * lp_band_far;
lp_band_far += lp_f_far * lp_high_far;
lp_low2_far += lp_f_far * lp_band2_far;
lp_high2_far = lp_low_far - lp_low2_far - lp_q * lp_band2_far;
lp_band2_far += lp_f_far * lp_high2_far;
heart_far_now = lp_low2_far * 1.5;

hb_dw = hb_delay_write & (hb_delay_buf_size - 1);
hb_dr = (hb_delay_write - hb_delay_samples) & (hb_delay_buf_size - 1);
hb_dw[0] = heart_far_now;
heart_far = hb_dr[0];
hb_delay_write = (hb_delay_write + 1) & (hb_delay_buf_size - 1);

hb_delay_sign == 1 ? (
  hb_l = heart_near; hb_r = heart_far;
) : (
  hb_l = heart_far; hb_r = heart_near;
);
hb_l *= slider14; hb_r *= slider14;

// --- BREATH ---
breath_state_pos += breath_state_advance;
breath_state_pos >= breath_state_len ? (
  breath_state_pos = 0;
  breath_state == 3 ? (
    // End of bottom pause — about to start a new breath.
    pr_br_enabled && !pr_br_resting ? (
      pr_br_breath += 1;
      pr_br_breath >= play_for_br ? (
        pr_br_resting = 1;
        pr_br_breath = 0;
        pr_br_rest_elapsed = 0;
      );
    );
    !pr_br_resting ? (
      // Sigh handling at the start of a new breath. Clear any previous
      // sigh first (it covered all 4 states of the breath we just finished),
      // then check the timer — if it's time, this NEW breath is the sigh.
      sigh_active = 0;
      slider32 > 0 && sigh_time_since_last >= slider32 * 60 ? (
        sigh_active = 1;
        sigh_time_since_last = 0;
      );
      breath_state     = 0;
      // Multiply by slider33 if sigh_active; otherwise use the
      // currently-drifted inhale_len as-is. Same pattern applies at every
      // state transition below — drift wanders the underlying segment
      // length, sigh scales it on top at the moment of state entry.
      breath_state_len = sigh_active ? max(floor(inhale_len * slider33), 1) : inhale_len;
    );
  ) : (
    breath_state = (breath_state + 1) % 4;
    // Each state's length is the currently-drifted segment length,
    // multiplied by slider33 if we're mid-sigh. sigh_active stays set
    // through all four states of the sigh breath; it clears at the
    // next 3->0 transition above.
    breath_state == 1 ? breath_state_len = sigh_active ? max(floor(top_len    * slider33), 1) : top_len;
    breath_state == 2 ? breath_state_len = sigh_active ? max(floor(exhale_len * slider33), 1) : exhale_len;
    breath_state == 3 ? breath_state_len = sigh_active ? max(floor(bot_len    * slider33), 1) : bot_len;
  );
);

pr_br_resting ? (
  pr_br_rest_duration_samples = rest_for_br * total_breath_samples;
  pr_br_rest_elapsed += breath_state_advance;
  pr_br_rest_elapsed >= pr_br_rest_duration_samples ? (
    pr_br_resting    = 0;
    pr_br_breath     = 0;
    // Rest-exit forces a fresh inhale — treat it as a new-breath
    // transition for sigh purposes too. Same sigh-fire logic as the
    // normal 3->0 path above.
    sigh_active = 0;
    slider32 > 0 && sigh_time_since_last >= slider32 * 60 ? (
      sigh_active = 1;
      sigh_time_since_last = 0;
    );
    breath_state     = 0;
    breath_state_len = sigh_active ? max(floor(inhale_len * slider33), 1) : inhale_len;
    breath_state_pos = 0;
  );
);

breath_phase_pos = breath_state_pos / max(breath_state_len - 1, 1);

hp_lp_l += hp_f * (n3 - hp_lp_l);
br_hp_l = n3 - hp_lp_l;
in_lp_l += in_f_l * in_bp_l;
in_hp2_l = br_hp_l - in_lp_l - breath_lp_q * in_bp_l;
in_bp_l += in_f_l * in_hp2_l;
ex_lp_l += ex_f_l * ex_bp_l;
ex_hp2_l = br_hp_l - ex_lp_l - breath_lp_q * ex_bp_l;
ex_bp_l += ex_f_l * ex_hp2_l;
hp_lp_r += hp_f * (n4 - hp_lp_r);
br_hp_r = n4 - hp_lp_r;
in_lp_r += in_f_r * in_bp_r;
in_hp2_r = br_hp_r - in_lp_r - breath_lp_q * in_bp_r;
in_bp_r += in_f_r * in_hp2_r;
ex_lp_r += ex_f_r * ex_bp_r;
ex_hp2_r = br_hp_r - ex_lp_r - breath_lp_q * ex_bp_r;
ex_bp_r += ex_f_r * ex_hp2_r;

(breath_state == 0) ? (
  br_env_raw = apply_fade(breath_phase_pos, slider24, slider25, slider28);
  br_env     = br_floor + (1.0 - br_floor) * br_env_raw;
  br_l = in_lp_l * br_env * 1.5;
  br_r = in_lp_r * br_env * 1.5;
) : (breath_state == 1) ? (
  br_l = in_lp_l * br_floor * 1.5;
  br_r = in_lp_r * br_floor * 1.5;
) : (breath_state == 2) ? (
  br_env_raw = apply_fade(breath_phase_pos, slider26, slider27, slider28);
  br_env     = br_floor + (1.0 - br_floor) * br_env_raw;
  br_l = ex_lp_l * br_env * 1.5;
  br_r = ex_lp_r * br_env * 1.5;
) : (
  br_l = ex_lp_l * br_floor * 1.5;
  br_r = ex_lp_r * br_floor * 1.5;
);
br_l *= slider35; br_r *= slider35;

bpf_low_l += bpf_f * bpf_band_l;
bpf_high_l = br_l - bpf_low_l - bpf_q * bpf_band_l;
bpf_band_l += bpf_f * bpf_high_l;
br_l = bpf_low_l;

bpf_low_r += bpf_f * bpf_band_r;
bpf_high_r = br_r - bpf_low_r - bpf_q * bpf_band_r;
bpf_band_r += bpf_f * bpf_high_r;
br_r = bpf_low_r;

// --- BLOODFLOW ---
// Bloodflow offset (2026-09-06). The pulse sits this far behind the heart
// sound, as a fraction of one cycle. Wrapped into [0,1) so an offset longer
// than a cycle lands in the next one, which is meaningful rather than an error
// -- the flow you hear belongs to the previous beat. Negative wraps the same
// way. At the default of 0 this is bit-identical to what it always did.
// Drift target 10 and Ramp target 10 wander the offset in the control's own
// units, exactly as every other target does, so they are added before the unit
// factor is applied rather than after.
bf_offset_cycles = (slider37 + target_drift_offset[10] + speed_ramp_bf_off_offset)
                   * bf_unit_to_cycles;
bf_cycle_pos = hb_phase / max(cycle_len, 1) - bf_offset_cycles;
bf_cycle_pos -= floor(bf_cycle_pos);

bf_cycle_pos < bf_attack ? (
  bf_t = bf_cycle_pos / bf_attack;
  bf_env_main = 0.5 - 0.5 * cos(bf_t * $pi);
) : bf_cycle_pos < (bf_attack + bf_decay) ? (
  bf_t = (bf_cycle_pos - bf_attack) / bf_decay;
  bf_env_main = 0.5 + 0.5 * cos(bf_t * $pi);
) : (
  bf_env_main = 0;
);

bf_dicrotic_center     = bf_attack + bf_decay * 0.7;
bf_dicrotic_half_width = bf_decay * 0.12;
bf_dicrotic_distance   = abs(bf_cycle_pos - bf_dicrotic_center);
bf_dicrotic_distance < bf_dicrotic_half_width ? (
  bf_dt = bf_dicrotic_distance / bf_dicrotic_half_width;
  bf_env_dicrotic = bf_dicrotic_level * (0.5 + 0.5 * cos(bf_dt * $pi));
) : (
  bf_env_dicrotic = 0;
);

bf_env_pulse = bf_env_main + bf_env_dicrotic;
bf_env       = bf_floor + (1.0 - bf_floor) * bf_env_pulse;

bf_cut   = bf_filter_hz * israte2;
bf_cut_l = (bf_cut * (1.0 + bf_stereo_width * 0.08));
bf_cut_r = (bf_cut * (1.0 - bf_stereo_width * 0.08));

bf_fb_l = bf_res + bf_res / (1.0 - bf_cut_l);
bf_n3_l = bf_n3_l + bf_cut_l * (n5 - bf_n3_l + bf_fb_l * (bf_n3_l - bf_n4_l));
bf_n4_l = bf_n4_l + bf_cut_l * (bf_n3_l - bf_n4_l);

bf_fb_r = bf_res + bf_res / (1.0 - bf_cut_r);
bf_n3_r = bf_n3_r + bf_cut_r * (n6 - bf_n3_r + bf_fb_r * (bf_n3_r - bf_n4_r));
bf_n4_r = bf_n4_r + bf_cut_r * (bf_n3_r - bf_n4_r);

bf_l = bf_n4_l * bf_env * slider45 * 12.0;
bf_r = bf_n4_r * bf_env * slider45 * 12.0;

target_bf_mix = pr_bf_enabled && pr_bf_resting ? 0 : 1;
bf_output_mix = bf_output_mix * pr_smooth + target_bf_mix * (1 - pr_smooth);
bf_l *= bf_output_mix;
bf_r *= bf_output_mix;

// --- SOLO LOGIC ---
any_solo = (slider15 > 0) || (slider36 > 0) || (slider46 > 0);
any_solo ? (
  hb_l = slider15  > 0 ? hb_l : 0;
  hb_r = slider15  > 0 ? hb_r : 0;
  br_l = slider36 > 0 ? br_l : 0;
  br_r = slider36 > 0 ? br_r : 0;
  bf_l = slider46 > 0 ? bf_l : 0;
  bf_r = slider46 > 0 ? bf_r : 0;
) : 0;

out_l = hb_l + br_l + bf_l;
out_r = hb_r + br_r + bf_r;

slider47 == 0 ? (
  spl0 = out_l; spl1 = out_r;
) : (
  spl0 = out_r; spl1 = out_l;
);

);  // close start-delay else branch

@serialize
// Persist per-target drift + Speed Ramp (by / duration / start-delay) configs
// so reload restores all targets, not just whichever was selected at save.
//
// VERSION-GUARDED (CLAUDE.md @serialize gotcha; NEW in v2.14 — womb v3's stream
// was previously unversioned). Magic = 2100000 + N_TARGETS marks the
// per-target-timeline format (added the duration + start-delay banks). Older
// (unversioned) blobs won't match and fall through to @init defaults — drift +
// ramp reset on upgrade (accepted; pre-release). Marker is float32-exact.
// Magic 2200000 marks heart-rate-as-BPM in Host x (it was a tempo multiplier
// under 2100000). The PAYLOAD is identical between the two, so both are read
// the same way and an upgrade keeps every drift and ramp config; only the
// migration flag differs. Accepting the old magic rather than falling through
// to defaults is the whole point -- a version bump that resets your settings
// is not a migration.
// 2400000 (2026-09-06). Two things changed at once, and BOTH need care:
//
//   * N_TARGETS went 10 -> 11 (Bloodflow offset appended). Every bank in this
//     stream is N_TARGETS wide, so an older blob's banks are TEN wide. Reading
//     one at eleven would consume a value too many from each and turn every
//     bank after the first into garbage. `n_ser` is the width to read, taken
//     from the magic. SIX of the nine instances in the library are on the
//     oldest blob, so this is not a theoretical path.
//   * Drift and Ramp play/rest banks were added. They are APPENDED at the end,
//     which is safe without any width games: an older stream simply runs out,
//     and a read past the end leaves the destination untouched and raises
//     nothing, so those banks keep their @init defaults of 0 -- which is "no
//     staircase", the correct reading for a project that never had one.
//
// The heart-rate seeding flags are gone with the block that used them; the
// 2026-09-06 layout migration does that conversion once, in the project file.
sr_magic = 2400000 + N_TARGETS;
file_var(0, sr_magic);
(sr_magic == 2400000 + N_TARGETS      || sr_magic == 2300000 + OLD_N_TARGETS ||
 sr_magic == 2200000 + OLD_N_TARGETS  || sr_magic == 2100000 + OLD_N_TARGETS) ? (
  n_ser = sr_magic == 2400000 + N_TARGETS ? N_TARGETS : OLD_N_TARGETS;
  file_mem(0, target_drift_up,    n_ser);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_down,  n_ser);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_per,   n_ser);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_shape, n_ser);
  file_var(0, last_target_select);
  file_mem(0, speed_ramp_target_value_mem, n_ser);
  file_mem(0, speed_ramp_dur_mem,          n_ser);
  file_mem(0, speed_ramp_delay_mem,        n_ser);
  file_var(0, last_speed_target);

  // Host-sync beats bank. RETIRED as a control, still read at 2300000 so the
  // stream stays aligned for anything after it. Nothing consumes the values.
  sr_magic == 2300000 + OLD_N_TARGETS ? (
    file_mem(0, host_beats_mem, HS_BANK_SLOTS);
    file_var(0, last_hs_sel);
  );

  // Drift and Ramp play/rest, appended 2026-09-06. Written only at the new
  // magic; at an older one these reads run past the end of the stream, which
  // leaves the banks at their @init zeroes. Asserted by reading: after adding a
  // bank, check it appears in BOTH this list and the duplicate-fix block below.
  sr_magic == 2400000 + N_TARGETS ? (
    file_mem(0, drift_play_mem, n_ser);
    file_mem(0, drift_rest_mem, n_ser);
    file_mem(0, ramp_play_mem,  n_ser);
    file_mem(0, ramp_rest_mem,  n_ser);
  );

  // TRACK-DUPLICATE FIX (v2.14). On READ (load/duplicate) file_avail(0) >= 0;
  // on WRITE it is < 0. @serialize is the only section guaranteed to run on
  // both load AND duplicate, so it re-establishes the bank<->slider invariant:
  // force the visible config sliders to match the restored banks for the
  // restored selectors, correcting any transient @slider clobber.
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
    last_speed_target  = min(N_TARGETS - 1, max(0, last_speed_target));
    last_target_select = min(N_TARGETS - 1, max(0, last_target_select));
    slider64 = speed_ramp_target_value_mem[last_speed_target]; // Ramp by
    slider66 = speed_ramp_dur_mem[last_speed_target];          // Ramp duration
    slider70 = speed_ramp_delay_mem[last_speed_target];        // Ramp start delay
    slider67 = ramp_play_mem[last_speed_target];               // Ramp play for
    slider68 = ramp_rest_mem[last_speed_target];               // Ramp rest for
    slider56 = target_drift_up[last_target_select];            // Drift up
    slider57 = target_drift_down[last_target_select];          // Drift down
    slider58 = target_drift_per[last_target_select];           // Drift period
    slider60 = target_drift_shape[last_target_select];         // Drift shape
    slider61 = drift_play_mem[last_target_select];             // Drift play for
    slider62 = drift_rest_mem[last_target_select];             // Drift rest for
    // sliderchange, NOT slider_automate: this is a restore, and automating it
    // would write automation the user never asked for.
    sliderchange(-1);
  );
);
